<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gingee</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Senji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gingee, also called Senji, is a town in Viluppuram district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, known for its great fortress spread over three granite hills. The fort was the seat of the Gingee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaks</a> from the early sixteenth century and later passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, whose king Rajaram held it against a long Mughal siege until 1698, before falling to the French and the British.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gingee.jpg" alt="Gingee" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KARTY JazZ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gingee_Fort_Hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gingee, originally called Singapuri, lies between three hills with a perimeter of three kilometres, west of the Sankaraparani River, at an average elevation of 92 metres. The nearest railway stations are at Tindivanam, Viluppuram and Thiruvannamalai, and the town is 147 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a> and 64 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a>. The earliest mention of the hill fort is an inscription of the reign of Vikrama Chola, in which a Kadava feudatory calls himself lord of Senji of the strong embattled fort.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> the area passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, which divided the Tamil country among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-nayak-dynasty">Nayaks of Madurai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Tanjore</a> and Gingee; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaks</a> strengthened the forts and enlarged the town. The last Nayak surrendered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Bijapur Sultanate</a>, which took booty said to be worth 20 crore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. In 1677 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> conquered the fort, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> greatly strengthened it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> took it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaram-i">Rajaram I</a> early in 1698 after a siege of seven years under Zulfikar Khan.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> later gave the fort to his Bundela Rajput servant Raja Swarup Singh. When his son Desingh refused to pay the arrears demanded by Sadatullah Khan, Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a>, he was killed in battle on 3 October 1714, aged 22, against a far larger army, and ballads about him are still sung locally. Daud Khan had already moved the provincial headquarters to Arcot, and Gingee declined. The French under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-de-bussy">Marquis de Bussy</a> took it on 11 September 1750 and held it until after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> carried it by assault in November 1780, and the English recovered it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> in 1799.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gingee is a panchayat town in Viluppuram district, with a population of 27,045 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and its fort draws visitors. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple below the fort dates from the time of Raja Desingh, and the nearby Mel Sithamur Jain Math is the main religious centre of the Tamil Jain community.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gingee” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bijapur annexed Bidar in 1619, and in the 1630s and 1640s its generals, among them the Maratha commander Shahaji Bhonsle, conquered much of the southern Karnataka plateau and parts of the Tamil country, taking Bangalore in 1638 and the fortress of <b>Gingee</b> in 1649.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Politically, the nayaka kingdoms that succeeded it, including Madurai, Thanjavur, <b>Gingee</b>, Ikkeri and the Wodeyars of Mysore, inherited its institutions and claimed its legitimacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1509 one line, traditionally founded by Tubaki Krishnappa Nayaka, was installed at <b>Gingee</b> (Senji), controlling the land between the Palar and Kollidam rivers, including the coast around Pondicherry.</blockquote><a href="/state/gingee-nayaks">Gingee Nayaks</a></li></ul></section>
